\section{Permutation resolution}{


\code{distmer.resolution_alpha} (default \code{0.05}) is the level a permutation
distribution's \emph{resolution} is judged against. A restricted permutation
scheme can attain many arrangements while producing only a handful of
distinct statistics -- permuting labels inside a treatment group leaves the
statistic untouched -- so the smallest p-value a design can return may sit
well above zero. Two groups of two attain 24 arrangements but only three
distinct pseudo-F values, and can never return a p-value below a third.

Every test specification records \code{distinct_statistics} and
\code{minimum_attainable_p} regardless of this option. The option decides only
when a design that cannot reach the chosen level says so in its warnings:
lower it to \code{0.01} to be told only about designs that cannot reach one
percent, or raise it to \code{0.1} to be warned more readily.
}

\section{Progress reporting}{


\code{distmer.progress} (default: whether the session is interactive) turns the
permutation progress bar on or off. \code{distmer.progress_min} (default \code{200})
is the number of permutations below which a run is too short to be worth
reporting. Both are ignored outside an interactive session unless
\code{distmer.progress} is set explicitly, so scripts, tests, vignettes, and
\verb{R CMD check} stay quiet.
}

